@@ -0,0 +1,119 @@
+# Demand forecasting module: implementation notes
+
+**Nothing here has been run.** There was no PHP or database to run against, so all of it is untested. The first thing to do is run `sql/updates/44.php` on a copy of a real database, and do a fresh install, then click through the pages.
+
+## What was built
+
+A new main-menu module **Forecasting** (`forecast`), placed straight after Manufacturing. It has 11 pages, 11 tables, a shared function library and the database changes for new and existing installations.
+
+### Pages
+
+All pages are in the repository root. Menu captions and security levels are registered in `scripts` and `menuitems`.
+
+| Page | Security | What it does |
+|---|---|---|
+| `ForecastDashboard.php` | 2 | Counts of active forecasts and active summary forecasts, forecast demands in MRP, the sales history range, links to every function (including the MRP pages). |
+| `ForecastHistory.php` | 11 | Sales history extraction for a month range (default is the last 24 complete months). Shows the range, items and months held, and the latest runs. |
+| `ForecastGenerate.php` | 11 | Forecast one item (optionally one location, otherwise all locations combined) or every active item of a category. Runs in batches of 20 items per request with a progress bar, then a "47 successful, 3 failed" summary with the reason for each failure. |
+| `ForecastReview.php` | 11 | Search by item, category or location. Period-by-period view with calculated quantity, a revised-quantity input, quantity used, last year's actual for the same month, and variance and variance %. Large variances are highlighted. A blank revised quantity restores the calculated one. |
+| `ForecastSummary.php`, `ForecastSummaryReview.php` | 11 | Summary forecasts by stock category, customer type, sales area or salesperson, with a review and revise screen and the detail behind each summary. |
+| `ForecastToMRP.php` | 11 | Converts active forecasts into MRP demands (replace or incremental mode). |
+| `ForecastInquiry.php` | 2 | Forecasts by item, category, location and period range. |
+| `ForecastAccuracy.php` | 2 | MAD, POA, MSE and RMSE per item, per category and per method for a month range. Items below the low-accuracy threshold are flagged. |
+| `ForecastMethodComparison.php` | 2 | One item, several methods side by side: hold-out accuracy, hold-out month by month, and the forward forecast. |
+| `ForecastParameters.php` | 15 | Company-wide defaults, administrator only. |
+
+Levels 2 and 11 are "Basic Reports and Inquiries" and "Inventory Management", both held by the Manufac/Stock Admin role. Level 15 is the administrator token.
+
+The calculations and shared data handling are in `includes/ForecastFunctions.php`.
+
+### Objects (tables)
+
+The create statements are in `install/sql/tables/forecast*.sql` for new installs and in `sql/updates/44.php` for upgrades.
+
+- `forecastparameters`: key/value defaults: alpha, beta, gamma, season length, moving-average periods, hold-out months, default horizon, plus a few I added (default percent, linear span, the two weight lists, low-accuracy POA threshold, variance highlight %).
+- `forecasthistoryruns`: one row per extraction (range, who, when). It is the source of the "range held" shown on the pages.
+- `forecasthistory`: item × location × month → net quantity. The primary key means re-extraction cannot double.
+- `forecasthistorydim`: the same sales totalled by stock category, customer type, area and salesperson, filled in the same pass. Summary forecasts read this table.
+- `forecasts` and `forecastdetails`: the forecast header (method, parameters as JSON, horizon, history end, notes, who and when) and one row per period with `calcqty` and `revisedqty`.
+- `forecastsummaries` and `forecastsummarydetails`: the same shape for summary forecasts.
+- `forecastbatches` and `forecastbatchresults`: the batch jobs behind the progress screen and the result list.
+- `forecastmrpdemands`: the link from each MRP demand the module writes (`demandid`) to its item and month.
+
+The **active** forecast is enforced by the database. `isactive` is 1 or NULL, with a unique key on (item, location, isactive), so at most one active forecast can exist per item and location. Generating again sets the old one to NULL (superseded, kept for accuracy comparison) and inserts the new one in one transaction. The same applies to summaries.
+
+`revisedqty` is NULL when there is no revision. 0 is a legitimate revision. Everything that reads quantities (review, inquiry, accuracy, MRP) goes through `ForecastEffectiveSQL()`, which is `COALESCE(revisedqty, calcqty)`.
+
+### Where the sales history comes from
+
+`ForecastExtractHistory()` reads `stockmoves` with `type IN (10, 11)` and `hidemovt = 0`, and sums `-qty` by item, location and month of `trandate`.
+
+- Type 10 is the customer invoice and type 11 the credit note, so credits are deducted.
+- This is the same set of movements that Inventory Item Usage (`StockUsage.php`) counts as sales, minus that report's adjustment and work-order movement types.
+- Open sales orders are not used. `MRPCreateDemands.php` works from `salesorders`, which is exactly what the BRD says not to do.
+- Re-running a range deletes the history rows in that range first, in the same transaction.
+- The range can only run to the last complete month. A partial month would read as a collapse in demand.
+- Months with no sales are not stored. The series for an item runs from its first month with sales to the end of the history held, and gaps in between count as zero. Months before the first sale are not zero-filled, so a new item is not penalised.
+
+### How a forecast reaches MRP
+
+1. `ForecastToMRP.php` takes a date horizon, optional category or item, and a mode.
+2. For each item it uses the active forecast over all locations if there is one. Otherwise it adds up the active per-location forecasts, so nothing is counted twice.
+3. It writes one `mrpdemands` row per month, quantity = revised quantity if present, else calculated, with demand type **`FC`** ("Statistical Forecast"). `FC` is created by the upgrade and by the installer. The due date is the first day of the month, or the horizon start for the first month. Zero quantities are skipped.
+4. Each row written is also recorded in `forecastmrpdemands`. That table is how the module knows which demands are its own.
+   - **Replace mode** deletes only recorded demands for the items in scope whose month falls in the horizon, then writes the new ones, in one transaction.
+   - **Incremental mode** only writes months that have no recorded forecast demand yet.
+   - Running either mode twice gives the same demands.
+   - Demands entered by hand on `MRPDemands.php`, from `MRPCreateDemands.php`, or by anything else are not in the link table and are never touched. This holds even if someone enters demands by hand under type `FC`.
+5. The existing `MRP.php` already loads every row of `mrpdemands` when "use master schedule demands" is ticked, so no change to MRP was needed. `MRPDemandTypes.php` now refuses to delete `FC`.
+
+The Forecasting menu also links Master Schedule, MRP Calculation and the MRP report.
+
+### Integration
+
+- **DB upgrade:** `sql/updates/44.php`. The highest file number is picked up automatically as `DBVersion`, so existing databases are sent to the upgrade page. It creates the tables, adds the module, scripts and menu items, adds demand type `FC`, and inserts the default parameters.
+- **New installs:** the tables are picked up from `install/sql/tables/*.sql`. The module, menu, scripts, `FC` type and parameters come from `install/sql/forecasting.sql`, which `InstallFunctions.php` runs after the base data in both the normal and the demo install. I used a separate file because `data/menuitems.sql`, `data/scripts.sql` and `demo.sql` are single enormous generated lines.
+- **User access:** modules are switched on per user by position in the comma list `www_users.modulesallowed`, in `modules.sequence` order. Forecasting takes position 7, so the update inserts a `0` at that position for every existing user and trims or pads each string to the 11 pre-existing flags first. Nobody gains or loses a module, and an administrator grants Forecasting in Users Maintenance, where "Display Forecasting module" now appears. This runs once only, guarded by whether the module row already existed. New users default to all modules on, as before. The installer's admin string already had 12 flags. `CustLoginSetup.php` now writes 12 flags and `SuppLoginSetup.php` has the module in its list.
+- **Menu rows:** `NewModule()`/`NewMenuItem()` and the install file add the rows for every security role, as the existing ones do. `index.php` hides items by page security.
+- **Translations:** all text uses `__()`, including the menu captions in `44.php`. The `.pot` and `.po` files are generated from source by the project's gettext tooling and I did not edit them, so the new strings will appear after the next extraction.
+
+## Methods
+
+All 12 are in `ForecastCompute()`. Forecasts are rounded to 2 decimals and never below 0. Short history gives a reported failure and never made-up data. A series is oldest first and "last year" means the most recent 12 months.
+
+| # | Method | Min months | Notes |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 1 | Percent over last year | 12 | Same month of the last 12 months × (1 + p/100). A horizon beyond 12 repeats last year's pattern. |
+| 2 | Calculated percent | 24 | Growth = last 12 months ÷ the 12 before, applied to last year. If the earlier year is zero or negative, it fails with that reason. |
+| 3 | Last year to this year | 12 | Same month last year. |
+| 4 | Moving average | max(4, N) | Flat forecast at the mean of the last N months. |
+| 5 | Linear approximation | 6 | Line through the latest month and the month "span" before it (default 5). |
+| 6 | Least squares | 12 | Straight line over all history. |
+| 7 | Second degree | 12 | Quadratic over all history, solved with centred time. |
+| 8 | Flexible | max(12, weights) | Up to 12 percentage weights per prior month, not normalised. Each forecast month is the weighted sum of the preceding months, and forecast months feed later ones. |
+| 9 | Weighted moving average | max(4, weights) | Default 40/30/20/10, normalised, flat. |
+| 10 | Simple exponential smoothing | 12 | Alpha 0.1–0.9. |
+| 11 | Holt | 12 | Alpha, beta. |
+| 12 | Holt-Winters | max(24, 2 × season) | Additive form, alpha, beta, gamma, season length (2–12, default 12). |
+
+**Best fit:** the last H months (default 6) are held out. Each method is trained on the rest and scored on the hold-out. For one item the winner has the lowest MAD, then the lowest RMSE, then the lower method number. For a category, each method is scored by total absolute error ÷ total actual sales over the hold-out, summed across the items. A method must be applicable to every item that can be tested at all, otherwise methods would be compared on different items. If none qualifies, the methods that run on the most items are compared. The chosen method is used for every item in the category.
+
+## Ambiguities in the BRD and how I resolved them
+
+- **Min history for the best fit.** I check it against the shorter training series, not the full one. With 24 months and a 6-month hold-out, Holt-Winters is not tested, because it would be trained on 18 months. Once the winner is chosen, the real forecast uses the full history.
+- **Moving average and weighted MA minimum.** The BRD says 4 months, but averaging N periods with fewer than N months would be a different calculation. The minimum is max(4, N).
+- **POA definition.** The BRD does not define it. I used 100 × (1 − Σ|error| ÷ Σactual), floored at 0, and 100 if nothing was sold and nothing forecast. The other common definition, Σforecast ÷ Σactual, lets over- and under-forecasts cancel, which is useless for flagging low accuracy.
+- **Accuracy and superseded forecasts.** The accuracy page measures the active forecasts by default and has an "Include superseded" box for the older versions. It measures the quantity used (revised first), and only months that now have actuals in the history.
+- **"Same sales the rest of webERP reports".** I took this as the invoiced/credited stock movements (see above). `salesanalysis` is a separate summary table that Utilities can re-apply, and I did not use it.
+- **Summary forecasts and customer dimensions.** The item/location history has no customer, so the extraction also keeps totals by category, customer type, area and salesperson, taken from the same movements. Customer type comes from the debtor, area and salesperson from the customer branch. Sales to unknown debtors go under "Unassigned". Summaries add up quantities in each item's own unit, and they do not go to MRP.
+- **Revisions on regeneration.** Generating again supersedes the old forecast and does not carry its revised quantities over.
+- **Locations.** '' means all locations combined. A per-location forecast is listed under the stock locations the user may view. The extraction itself is company-wide and not limited by user.
+- **Active items.** For category batches "active" means not discontinued and not a dummy (`mbflag = 'D'`) item. An item with no history fails with that reason.
+- **Parameter ranges.** Alpha is held to 0.1–0.9 as the BRD says. Beta and gamma are accepted from 0.01 to 0.99, and the BRD gives no range for them.
+
+## Known limits and things to check
+
+- `install/sql/tables/*.sql` still show `stockid varchar(20)`, while update 43 widened it to 64. My tables use `varchar(64)` and no foreign keys to `stockmaster`, so they work either way. I did not touch the older install files.
+- `modulesallowed` is `varchar(25)`, which holds 12 flags (24 characters). A thirteenth module would need the column widened.
+- Batch generation re-runs the category best-fit evaluation in one request at the start, which could be slow on a very large category. Items are then forecast 20 per request.
+- Incremental MRP mode does not update a month that was converted earlier and has since been revised. Use replace mode for that.
